{
  "decline_reason": "",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Section 43 makes a trade mark’s connotation relevant where its use for the designated goods or services would be likely to deceive or cause confusion.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s43",
          "quote": "An application for the registration of a trade mark in respect of particular goods or services must be rejected if, because of some connotation that the trade mark or a sign contained in the trade mark has, the use of the trade mark in relation to those goods or services would be likely to deceive or cause confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "As reported in the Manual, Katzmann J in Primary Health Care Limited v Commonwealth of Australia [2017] FCAFC 174 expressly distinguished denotation from connotation on the basis that “connotation” in section 43 bears its ordinary meaning.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/2/2/1~1",
          "quote": "Denotation and connotation are also discussed in Primary Health Care Limited v Commonwealth of Australia [2017] FCAFC 174. Katzmann J at 111:"
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/2/2/1~2",
          "quote": "plainly, if “connotation” in s 43 is to bear its ordinary meaning (and the respondents did not argue otherwise), then a distinction is to be made between the denotation of the mark and its connotation."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "In that account, Katzmann J approved treating the denotation of “Primary Health Care” as a trade mark connection between the applicant and the designated services, qualifying the primary judge’s reference to the applicant’s medical centres.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/2/2/1~2",
          "quote": "In my respectful opinion, subject to the qualification that “Primary Health Care” denotes a connection between the applicant and the designated services (rather than a connection between the applicant and its medical centres), her Honour was correct to do so."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The contrasting connotation identified by the primary judge was that the applicant provides “first level or first contact health care”.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/2/2/1~2",
          "quote": "Her Honour went on to hold (at [164]) that the connotation in the marks is that the applicant provides “first level or first contact health care”."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual also reports that, in Bavaria NV v Bayerischer Brauerbund eV [2009] FCA 428, other elements of the mark neutralised a geographical connotation, illustrating the importance of considering the whole mark when identifying what it connotes.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/3/3/5",
          "quote": "When determining the potential connotation within a trade mark, the trade mark as a whole should be considered."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/3/3/5",
          "quote": "This issue was considered in Bavaria NV v Bayerischer Brauerbund eV [2009] FCA 428, where Bennet J took into account other elements that appeared as part of the trade mark in question, stating at 89: ‘….the reasonable consumer would not fail to observe the three references to “Holland”, which at least neutralise the connotation with the State of Bavaria’."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "For examination purposes, the Manual cautions against treating connotation as limited to indirect implications: it states that connotation under section 43 has a wide meaning and includes direct statements.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/2/2/1~1",
          "quote": "In respect of section 43, connotation has a wide meaning beyond the above definitions and includes direct statements."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    }
  ],
  "declined": false,
